> >Yes, the two 'e's in Ente are the same as the second 'e' in eben.

Hmm, the two 'e' in "Ente" aren't quite the same for me[x], while the
second 'e' in "eben" is ground down to one of those click sounds,
unless i'm really sloppy and make it "ehmt". There _are_ some dialects
which actually pronounce the second "e" in "eben", but this is not the
norm everywhere.

[x] the first has a very slight Umlaut-a coloration, the second is
closer to Umlaut-o. Might be my special vernacular.

> *blink* *blink*
> 
> I'll admit: almost all the German I've heard in recent years has been
> uttered by alternative musicians (mostly the bands Einstuerzende
> Neubauten, Das Ich, and Rammstein),

My condolences. Although i only really dislike Rammstein. For
something really out of the ordinary, there's a rather weird band from
Switzerland named "Les Reines prochaines", who sings mostly in german.
It's on the folkish fringe of alternative, so have a care. There's a
lot of german hip-hop, too, from the very old-school Die Fantastischen
Vier via the pseudo-gangsta Die Firma to the loopy-alternative Fettes
Brot.

> but from these, there definitely
> seem to be two different ways to pronounce 'en', depending on where it
> appears:
> 
> at the end of a word: usually with the 'e' being a schwa, as in "sie
> mussen sich".
> followed by another consonant: usually with the 'e' being stressed, as
> in "unendlich" and "letztendlich".

This gets compounded by the fact that in the latter two cases, there's
a minimal stop in front of the "e" which is at the beginning of a new
syllable (try to pronounce "un-endlich" vs. "u-nendlich"), unlike in
e.g. "Mensch".

And there's the tendency to drop an unpronounced "e" followed by a
consonant in german. When i pronounce "Schwalbenschwanz"
(swallow-tail, a very beautiful butterfly) without taking an effort to
be understandable, it only has two syllables, and the first is
something like "Schwal[b]m" (that's not a proper "b", but the strange
sound you get when trying to pronounce one with a closed mouth, a sort
of nasal plosive). 

> Terminal 'e' preceded by a consonant seems to be almost always a schwa,
> as in "hier kommt die Sonne".
> 
> (I'm perfectly prepared to believe that Blixa Bargeld and Till Lindemann
> have appalling German diction; they are alternative musicians, after
> all.)

Ah no, they're speaking perfect stage german, for all that i can say.
Nice rolled "r" (which is a back-of-the-throat rasping sound with me),
proper "i" in "wirst" (which frequently turns into either "wierst"
[common in north-west germany] or "würst" [common in the more eastern
parts; my most common pronunciation sin]), no swallowed endings. It's
a special dialect of german which you rarely hear outside of theatres
and bad german films.

